    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61803,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 44,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovary</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:55:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61804,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 44,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients present with an abdominal mass or pain. Some may have abnormal vaginal bleeding {3570630; 4005816}. Tumours are usually unilateral (&gt;&nbsp;80%) and occasionally bilateral {3570630; 27130406}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:55:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61805,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 44,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>According to pathological case series, malignant Brenner tumours account for &lt;&nbsp;5% of all Brenner tumours {5040741; 2321577}. These tumours occur in women aged &gt;&nbsp;50&nbsp;years.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:55:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61806,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 44,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:55:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61807,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 44,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Malignant Brenner tumours arise from benign and borderline Brenner tumours, but key molecular alterations and the exact pathway have not yet been elucidated. <em>PIK3CA</em> mutations and <em>MDM2</em> amplification have been reported, but mutations in the <em>TERT</em> promoter or <em>TP53</em> have not been found {19033864; 28639280; 27299795}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:55:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61808,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 44,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours have a median size of 10&nbsp;cm {27130406}. They may be solid or cystic with mural nodules.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:55:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61809,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 44,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The tumour is composed of irregularly shaped masses of cytologically atypical transitional/urothelial-type cells, sometimes with focal squamous differentiation. Cystic areas within the tumour are lined by multilayered epithelium, which resembles invasive urothelial carcinoma with hyperchromatic pleomorphic nuclei, variably prominent nucleoli, dense eosinophilic cytoplasm, and prominent mitotic activity. Invasion may be difficult to detect because of the densely fibromatous background of the tumour, but a desmoplastic stromal reaction is helpful in identifying unequivocal stromal invasion. Rarely, the invasive component appears to arise directly from a benign Brenner tumour, without an atypical proliferative (borderline) component {23202779}. Mucinous glandular elements and (more rarely) mucinous adenocarcinoma may coexist within the Brenner component. The absence of a benign or borderline Brenner component should raise the possibility of high-grade serous or endometrioid carcinoma with transitional cell&ndash;like differentiation {23018212; 23108017}.</p>\r\n<p><em>Immunohistochemistry</em></p>\r\n<p>These tumours are usually negative for WT1, negative or weakly positive for ER and PR, and focally positive for p16, showing a wildtype pattern for <em>TP53</em> {23018212; 19033864}. The data for GATA3 and p63 are very limited, because only one case was published and negative for GATA3, in contrast to benign and borderline Brenner tumours, which were positive. p63 was positive in all Brenner tumours {19092634}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:55:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61810,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 44,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:55:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61811,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 44,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:55:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61812,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 44,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> malignant tumour with urothelial differentiation; a benign or borderline Brenner tumour component in the background.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:55:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61813,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 44,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "<p>Malignant Brenner tumour is staged according to the Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) TNM classification (see <em>TNM staging of ovarian, fallopian tube, and primary peritoneal carcinoma</em>&nbsp;[[Brierley JD, Gospodarowicz MK, Wittekind C, editors. TNM classification of malignant tumours. 8th ed. Oxford (UK): Wiley-Blackwell; 2017.]]) and the FIGO staging system.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-15T11:09:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61814,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 44,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most tumours are confined to the ovary at diagnosis {27130406}. Patients with stage&nbsp;I tumours have a disease-specific 5-year survival rate of 94.5%; this drops to 51.3% in patients with extraovarian spread (stages&nbsp;II, III, IV) {27130406}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:55:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]